\details{
Two or more models are supported. Models can use identical, partially
overlapping, or completely disjoint samples. When the model calls refer to
the same data source, model-frame row names identify overlapping
observations. Models fitted from different data objects are treated as
disjoint by default because shared observations cannot be inferred safely.
Use \code{observation_id} to identify their common observations explicitly.
\code{suest()} warns when such models share row names with identical values, and
stops when models fit to the same data object disagree on rows with the same
row names (for example, after the data were filtered and renumbered).

When \code{newdata} is omitted in \code{marginaleffects} functions, each model is
averaged over its own estimation sample, as with
\code{newdata = suest_newdata(fit)}.

All combinations of the supported scalar-response models can be combined,
including models whose response variables or response scales differ. All
combinations of the supported categorical-response models can be combined,
and scalar and categorical models may appear in the same system. Results on
different response scales are labeled separately for \code{marginaleffects}.

Linear models include an ancillary \code{lnvar} parameter, the log of the error
variance (\code{log(RSS / df.residual)} for unweighted fits). It has no effect on
predictions or marginal effects.
Negative-binomial models include \code{log(theta)} in the joint parameter vector.
Ordered and multinomial models use analytic score and observed-information
calculations for stable robust covariance estimation.
Probit and other GLMs with non-canonical links, and beta regressions, use
observed information in the sandwich bread, as Stata does.

Aliased parameters are not supported. Nonunit weights are rejected unless
\code{weight_type = "pweight"}. Pweights must be finite and
strictly positive. For observations included in any pair of models, evaluated
weights must agree; weights may differ for observations unique to either
model, including completely disjoint samples. Pweight support is available
for linear, binary logit/probit, Poisson, negative-binomial, ordered
logit/probit, and multinomial logit models.

Bias-reduced, adjusted-score, Firth, and penalized
GLM fits are rejected because they do not use ordinary maximum-likelihood
score equations. Ordinary \code{glm} and \code{glm2} fits must have converged.
Penalized \code{nnet::multinom} fits with nonzero \code{decay} are also rejected.
Beta regression requires \code{type = "ML"}; bias-reduced and bias-corrected
beta fits and penalized \code{survreg} fits are not supported. Conventional
\code{survreg} fits with \code{robust = TRUE} use their model-based information for
the joint sandwich covariance.
}
\section{Survey models}{

Survey support combines linear, binary, and Poisson \code{svyglm()} fits,
ordered logit and probit \code{svyolr()} fits, multinomial logit,
negative-binomial, and generalized ordered \code{svy_vglm()} fits, tobit,
interval, and parametric survival \code{svysurvreg()} fits, and two-stage least
squares \code{svyivreg()} fits under one common one-stage design, in any
combination. Strata and first-stage finite-population corrections are
supported. Model-specific subsets and missing outcomes are aligned using
observation IDs, with zero influence outside each model's estimation sample.
The full design retains PSUs outside all model samples. Each model's native
design-based covariance must be reproduced from its scores before the joint
matrix is returned. Probit and cloglog \code{svyglm()} fits and \code{svyolr()},
\code{svy_vglm()}, and \code{svysurvreg()} models then use exact observed information,
as Stata does: \code{svyglm()} and VGAM use expected information, \code{svyolr()} a
numerical Hessian, and survival's scale residuals are wrong for
interval-censored outcomes, so their own standard errors can differ. \code{svyglm()} fits contain coefficients only; \code{svysurvreg()}
fits add \code{Log(scale)}, and negative-binomial fits the log size parameter.

Replicate-weight, multistage, two-phase, calibrated, raked, post-stratified,
and PPS designs are unsupported. Lonely-PSU options are restricted to
\code{fail}, \code{remove}, or \code{certainty}, with \code{survey.adjust.domain.lonely = FALSE}.
A singleton stratum with a certainty FPC is supported under \code{fail}.
Extra fitting weights are unsupported; place offsets in the model formula.

Predictions and effects use the joint design-based coefficient covariance.
Averaging treats the supplied covariate distribution as fixed; it does not
add design uncertainty from estimating that distribution. Use
\code{suest_newdata()} and \code{wts = ".suest_weight"} for model-specific weighted
averages. Inference uses the usual asymptotic normal default. The full
design's degrees of freedom are recorded in \verb{$survey$design_df}; no automatic
survey t or F adjustment is applied.
}

